(Unit tests for voxelf's header writers and its layout.

 The expected bytes are written out by hand from executables ld linked for
 two example programs, hello.vox and pi.vox, built by Vox 0.4.16: the ELF
 header of the first, and two program headers of the second. Each writer is
 handed the values those headers hold and must give back the same bytes.)

see "../../../tools/voxelf/voxelf.vox".

a number called passes is 0.
a number called failures is 0.

(Compares what a writer wrote with what it should have written, byte for
 byte, and names the first byte that differs.)
To 'expect the bytes' with a text called 'what was written' and a buffer called written and a list called expected.
    a number called 'bytes wanted' is expected's length.
    a number called 'bytes written' is written's size.
    If 'bytes written' is not 'bytes wanted' then,
        Print "FAIL {what was written}: {bytes written} bytes written, {bytes wanted} expected",
        increment failures,
        Return.
    For each position from 1 to 'bytes wanted',
        a number called 'byte wanted' is element position of expected,
        a number called 'byte written' is byte position of written,
        If 'byte written' is not 'byte wanted' then,
            Print "FAIL {what was written}: byte {position} is {byte written:02x}, expected {byte wanted:02x}",
            increment failures,
            Return..
    increment passes.

To 'expect the number' with a text called 'what was worked out' and a number called answer and a number called 'answer wanted'.
    If answer is 'answer wanted' then, increment passes, Return.
    Print "FAIL {what was worked out}: {answer:x}, expected {answer wanted:x}".
    increment failures.

(hello's ELF header: entry 0x4001df, three program headers, and seven
 section headers starting 5400 bytes into the file.)
Create a buffer called 'elf header'.
'append the elf header' with 'elf header' and 0x4001DF and 3 and 5400 and 7.
'expect the bytes' with "the ELF header" and 'elf header' and [
    0x7F, 0x45, 0x4C, 0x46, 0x02, 0x01, 0x01, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00,
    0x02, 0x00, 0x3E, 0x00, 0x01, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0xDF, 0x01, 0x40, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00,
    0x40, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x18, 0x15, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00,
    0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x40, 0x00, 0x38, 0x00, 0x03, 0x00, 0x40, 0x00, 0x07, 0x00, 0x06, 0x00].

(pi's writable segment: read and write, from offset 0x2038 and address
 0x403038, 0x2a8 bytes of file and 0x10b50 of memory, on page alignment.)
Create a buffer called 'data segment header'.
'append a program header' with 'data segment header' and 'the loadable segment type' and 'read and write' and 0x2038 and 0x403038 and 0x2A8 and 0x10B50 and 0x1000.
'expect the bytes' with "the writable segment's program header" and 'data segment header' and [
    0x01, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x06, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x38, 0x20, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00,
    0x38, 0x30, 0x40, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x38, 0x30, 0x40, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00,
    0xA8, 0x02, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x50, 0x0B, 0x01, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00,
    0x00, 0x10, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00].

(pi's stack header: PT_GNU_STACK, read and write, nothing else but an
 alignment of 16.)
Create a buffer called 'stack header'.
'append a program header' with 'stack header' and 'the stack segment type' and 'read and write' and 0 and 0 and 0 and 0 and 16.
'expect the bytes' with "the stack's program header" and 'stack header' and [
    0x51, 0xE5, 0x74, 0x64, 0x06, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00,
    0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00,
    0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00,
    0x10, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00].

(hello's sections, with the alignments ld's input gives them, placed as ld
 places them: the code after the three program headers at the next multiple
 of 16, the data on the next page, and the zero-filled data after it in
 memory, at the file offset where the data's bytes stop.)
Create a buffer called diagnostics.
a list called 'hello sections' is [
    {"name": ".text", "kind": "text", "size": 303, "alignment": 16},
    {"name": ".data", "kind": "data", "size": 102, "alignment": 4},
    {"name": ".bss", "kind": "bss", "size": 32, "alignment": 4}].
a list called placed is 'place the sections' with 'hello sections' and 0x400000 and diagnostics.
'expect the number' with "how many sections were placed" and placed's length and 3.
a map called 'placed text' is element 1 of placed.
a map called 'placed data' is element 2 of placed.
a map called 'placed zeros' is element 3 of placed.
'expect the number' with "the address of .text" and 'placed text''s "address" and 0x4000F0.
'expect the number' with "the file offset of .text" and 'placed text''s "file offset" and 0xF0.
'expect the number' with "the address of .data" and 'placed data''s "address" and 0x401000.
'expect the number' with "the file offset of .data" and 'placed data''s "file offset" and 0x1000.
'expect the number' with "the address of .bss" and 'placed zeros''s "address" and 0x401068.
'expect the number' with "the file offset of .bss" and 'placed zeros''s "file offset" and 0x1066.

(Read-only data gets a segment of its own, between the code and the data,
 and so a fourth program header, which moves the code along by 56 bytes. The
 read-only data starts a fresh page of the file, apart from the code; the
 data follows it in the file at its own alignment of 8, with no page of
 zeros between them, and starts in memory on the next page, at the same
 place in the page. The sizes are those of the ld build whose writable
 segment the program header above was written out from, and the whole
 header is compared again, built this time from the layout.)
a list called 'pi sections' is [
    {"name": ".text", "kind": "text", "size": 7328, "alignment": 16},
    {"name": ".rodata", "kind": "rodata", "size": 52, "alignment": 4},
    {"name": ".data", "kind": "data", "size": 680, "alignment": 8},
    {"name": ".bss", "kind": "bss", "size": 67752, "alignment": 4}].
a list called 'pi placed' is 'place the sections' with 'pi sections' and 0x400000 and diagnostics.
a map called 'pi text' is element 1 of 'pi placed'.
a map called 'pi read-only data' is element 2 of 'pi placed'.
a map called 'pi data' is element 3 of 'pi placed'.
a map called 'pi zeros' is element 4 of 'pi placed'.
'expect the number' with "the address of pi's .text" and 'pi text''s "address" and 0x400120.
'expect the number' with "the address of pi's .rodata" and 'pi read-only data''s "address" and 0x402000.
'expect the number' with "the file offset of pi's .rodata" and 'pi read-only data''s "file offset" and 0x2000.
'expect the number' with "the address of pi's .data" and 'pi data''s "address" and 0x403038.
'expect the number' with "the file offset of pi's .data" and 'pi data''s "file offset" and 0x2038.
'expect the number' with "the address of pi's .bss" and 'pi zeros''s "address" and 0x4032E0.
'expect the number' with "the file offset of pi's .bss" and 'pi zeros''s "file offset" and 0x22E0.
a list called 'pi segments' is 'the segments of' with 'pi placed' and 0x400000 and 0x120.
'expect the number' with "how many loadable segments pi has" and 'pi segments''s length and 3.
a map called 'pi writable segment' is element 3 of 'pi segments'.
Create a buffer called 'laid out data segment header'.
'append a program header' with 'laid out data segment header' and 'the loadable segment type' and 'pi writable segment''s "permissions" and 'pi writable segment''s "file offset" and 'pi writable segment''s "address" and 'pi writable segment''s "file size" and 'pi writable segment''s "memory size" and 'the page size'.
'expect the bytes' with "the writable segment's program header, from the layout" and 'laid out data segment header' and [
    0x01, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x06, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x38, 0x20, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00,
    0x38, 0x30, 0x40, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x38, 0x30, 0x40, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00,
    0xA8, 0x02, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x50, 0x0B, 0x01, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00,
    0x00, 0x10, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00].
a map called 'pi read-only segment' is element 2 of 'pi segments'.
'expect the number' with "the file offset of pi's read-only segment" and 'pi read-only segment''s "file offset" and 0x2000.
'expect the number' with "the address of pi's read-only segment" and 'pi read-only segment''s "address" and 0x402000.

(Zero-filled data with no initialised data before it opens the writable
 segment itself. It starts in memory on the page after the read-only data
 at the place the file had got to, 0x34, and its alignment of 8 moves it on
 to 0x38. The segment holds no bytes, and its file offset only has to agree
 with its address modulo the page size: ld gives it the address's place in
 its page, 0x38. ld pads .bss to a multiple of 8, so the 100 bytes take 104
 of memory.)
a list called 'zeros after read-only data' is [
    {"name": ".text", "kind": "text", "size": 7328, "alignment": 16},
    {"name": ".rodata", "kind": "rodata", "size": 52, "alignment": 4},
    {"name": ".bss", "kind": "bss", "size": 100, "alignment": 8}].
a list called 'zeros placed' is 'place the sections' with 'zeros after read-only data' and 0x400000 and diagnostics.
a map called 'the lone zeros' is element 3 of 'zeros placed'.
'expect the number' with "the address of zero-filled data after read-only data" and 'the lone zeros''s "address" and 0x403038.
a list called 'zeros segments' is 'the segments of' with 'zeros placed' and 0x400000 and 0x120.
a map called 'zeros segment' is element 3 of 'zeros segments'.
'expect the number' with "the address of a segment of zero-filled data" and 'zeros segment''s "address" and 0x403038.
'expect the number' with "the file offset of a segment of zero-filled data" and 'zeros segment''s "file offset" and 0x38.
'expect the number' with "the file size of a segment of zero-filled data" and 'zeros segment''s "file size" and 0.
'expect the number' with "the memory size of a segment of zero-filled data" and 'zeros segment''s "memory size" and 104.

(The same, with zero-filled data aligned to a page: its address's place in
 its page is 0, and ld gives such a segment the file offset of one page.)
a list called 'paged zeros' is [
    {"name": ".text", "kind": "text", "size": 7328, "alignment": 16},
    {"name": ".rodata", "kind": "rodata", "size": 52, "alignment": 4},
    {"name": ".bss", "kind": "bss", "size": 100, "alignment": 4096}].
a list called 'paged zeros placed' is 'place the sections' with 'paged zeros' and 0x400000 and diagnostics.
a list called 'paged zeros segments' is 'the segments of' with 'paged zeros placed' and 0x400000 and 0x120.
a map called 'paged zeros segment' is element 3 of 'paged zeros segments'.
'expect the number' with "the address of a page-aligned segment of zero-filled data" and 'paged zeros segment''s "address" and 0x404000.
'expect the number' with "the file offset of a page-aligned segment of zero-filled data" and 'paged zeros segment''s "file offset" and 0x1000.

(Data aligned to a page after 8 bytes of read-only data, which ends at
 0x402008. Started after that page at the same place, 0x403008, the data
 would be aligned on to 0x404000 and end at 0x404008, using part of the
 page at 0x403000 and part of the page at 0x404000, parts that fit in one
 page together. ld starts it on the fresh page at 0x403000 instead, a page
 less of memory, and the file is padded to its next page, 0x3000.)
a list called 'paged data' is [
    {"name": ".text", "kind": "text", "size": 7328, "alignment": 16},
    {"name": ".rodata", "kind": "rodata", "size": 8, "alignment": 4},
    {"name": ".data", "kind": "data", "size": 8, "alignment": 4096}].
a list called 'paged data placed' is 'place the sections' with 'paged data' and 0x400000 and diagnostics.
a map called 'the paged data' is element 3 of 'paged data placed'.
'expect the number' with "the address of page-aligned data after read-only data" and 'the paged data''s "address" and 0x403000.
'expect the number' with "the file offset of page-aligned data after read-only data" and 'the paged data''s "file offset" and 0x3000.

(The last page of the lower half of the address space that Linux gives a
 program, 0x7fffffffe000 to 0x7ffffffff000: code placed there is placed as
 from any other base.)
a list called 'one page of code' is [
    {"name": ".text", "kind": "text", "size": 12, "alignment": 16}].
a list called 'top page placed' is 'place the sections' with 'one page of code' and 0x7FFFFFFFE000 and diagnostics.
'expect the number' with "how many sections were placed on the last page" and 'top page placed''s length and 1.
a map called 'top page text' is element 1 of 'top page placed'.
'expect the number' with "the address of code on the last page" and 'top page text''s "address" and 0x7FFFFFFFE0B0.

(The upper half of the address space is placed as the lower half is. Vox
 holds its 64 bits as the number below zero with the same bits, as no
 literal can spell them: a kernel's base, 0xffffffff80000000, is 0x80000000
 below zero, and the top page, 0xfffffffffffff000, is a page below zero.
 Code from the top page that fills it, the 0xb0 bytes of the headers and
 3920 more, ends exactly at 2^64, which ld takes.)
a list called 'kernel code placed' is 'place the sections' with 'one page of code' and {0 subtract 0x80000000} and diagnostics.
'expect the number' with "how many sections were placed from a kernel's base" and 'kernel code placed''s length and 1.
a map called 'kernel text' is element 1 of 'kernel code placed'.
'expect the number' with "the address of code from a kernel's base" and 'kernel text''s "address" and {0 subtract 0x80000000 add 0xB0}.
a list called 'a page of code from the headers' is [
    {"name": ".text", "kind": "text", "size": 3920, "alignment": 16}].
a list called 'code to 2^64 placed' is 'place the sections' with 'a page of code from the headers' and {0 subtract 4096} and diagnostics.
'expect the number' with "how many sections were placed to end at 2^64" and 'code to 2^64 placed''s length and 1.

(Memory that does not lie wholly in one half is refused, each with one
 diagnostic: a base at 2^47, the first address that is not canonical, and
 one just under 2^63, both between the halves; zero-filled data from the
 last page and from the last page but one of the lower half, which would
 run into the addresses between the halves; and zero-filled data, or one
 byte of code too many, from the top page of the upper half, which would
 run past 2^64 and wrap round to 0.)
To 'expect a refused base' with a text called 'what was placed' and a list called sections and a number called 'load base'.
    Create a buffer called 'base complaints'.
    a list called 'base placed' is 'place the sections' with sections and 'load base' and 'base complaints'.
    'expect the number' with "how many sections were placed {what was placed}" and 'base placed''s length and 0.
    'expect the number' with "how many complaints {what was placed} drew" and {'the number of diagnostics in' with 'base complaints'} and 1.

a list called 'code and zeros' is [
    {"name": ".text", "kind": "text", "size": 12, "alignment": 16},
    {"name": ".bss", "kind": "bss", "size": 0x10000, "alignment": 16}].
a list called 'a byte too much code' is [
    {"name": ".text", "kind": "text", "size": 3921, "alignment": 16}].
'expect a refused base' with "from 2^47" and 'one page of code' and 0x800000000000.
'expect a refused base' with "from just under 2^63" and 'code and zeros' and 0x7FFFFFFFFFFFF000.
'expect a refused base' with "into the hole from the last page" and 'code and zeros' and 0x7FFFFFFFF000.
'expect a refused base' with "into the hole from the last page but one" and 'code and zeros' and 0x7FFFFFFFE000.
'expect a refused base' with "past 2^64 from the top page" and 'code and zeros' and {0 subtract 4096}.
'expect a refused base' with "a byte past 2^64 from the top page" and 'a byte too much code' and {0 subtract 4096}.

(A layout that cannot be honoured is refused, with one diagnostic for each
 thing wrong and the error flag raised.)
Create a buffer called complaints.
a list called 'bad sections' is [
    {"name": ".data", "kind": "data", "size": 8, "alignment": 3},
    {"name": ".code", "kind": "code", "size": 8, "alignment": 16}].
a boolean called 'the flag went up' is false.
a list called refused is 'place the sections' with 'bad sections' and 0x400000 and complaints.
On error set 'the flag went up' to true.
'expect the number' with "how many bad sections were placed" and refused's length and 0.
a number called 'complaints made' is 'the number of diagnostics in' with complaints.
'expect the number' with "how many complaints there were" and 'complaints made' and 3.
If 'the flag went up' then, increment passes.
Otherwise, Print "FAIL the error flag stayed down after a refused layout", increment failures.

Print "voxelf headers and layout: {passes} passed, {failures} failed".
If failures is greater than 0 then, Exit 1.
